     "type": "text",
     "text": "{\"count\": 2, \"rationale\": \"Deploying mfa:admin and mfa:dbadmin together blocks every exfiltration path. With mfa_admin, auth_dc and auth_adfs are blocked, so admin_dc and admin_adfs can never be reached. With mfa_dbadmin and no admin_dc or admin_adfs, auth_db, auth_fileserver and auth_backup are all blocked. These are the only ways to get a foothold on db, fileserver or backup, so exfiltrated is unreachable. No single control works. Phishing always gives foothold_workstation and stolen_user. Without segmented, the attacker pivots internally. With segmented alone, escalate_workstation gives admin_workstation, which allows the pivot. Once internal, auth_appserver or auth_ciserver bypasses mfa_user via foothold_workstation, and kerberoasting yields stolen_admin. escalate_ciserver then gives admin_ciserver, and leak_secrets_ciserver gives stolen_dbadmin. With mfa_admin alone, auth_db is open using stolen_dbadmin. With mfa_dbadmin alone, the DC path (auth_dc, escalate_dc) gives admin_dc, which bypasses mfa_dbadmin. Patches do not block any auth technique. So the minimum is 2.\"}"
    }
   ],
